Found a real bug in createRagdoll:
```
const clone = part.clone(true);
clone.matrix.copy(part.matrixWorld);
clone.matrix.decompose(...)
clone.traverse(o => {
  if (o.isMesh) {
    o.material = o.material.clone();
    ...
```
The error "Converting circular structure to JSON ... at JSON.stringify ... at Dn.copy ..." suggests `part.clone(true)` is cloning userData containing the enemy reference (o.userData.enemy = z, circular), and Three's Object3D.copy does JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(source.userData)). Indeed Object3D.copy does `this.userData = JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(source.userData))`. With circular userData (userData.enemy -> z -> root -> mesh.userData.enemy), it throws. This means *any* zombie kill throws and the ragdoll fails! Critical bug.

Fix: before cloning, clear enemy references from part userData (or clone without userData). Simplest: temporarily null out userData.enemy on all meshes in the part, clone, then restore? Or create clone manually. Since userData on meshes contains `{enemy: z, zone}`. We can set `o.userData.enemy = null` before cloning; but would that break rendering? userData.enemy is used for hit detection. During ragdoll creation, the zombie is already removed; restoring afterward not necessary. But we iterate part.clone(true) for each part; if we clear on the original before cloning, subsequent parts unaffected. We can just clear all enemy refs at the start of killEnemy before createRagdoll. But parts' meshes userData includes enemy only via model.root.traverse in spawnEnemy, setting o.userData.enemy=z on each mesh. After mergeLocalMeshes, the merged meshes have `userData = {zone}` (line 1428) without enemy, but spawnEnemy sets enemy on them. So yes.

Simplest robust fix inside createRagdoll:
```
const saved = [];
part.traverse(o=>{ if (o.userData && o.userData.enemy) { saved.push([o, o.userData.enemy]); o.userData.enemy = null; }});
const clone = part.clone(true);
for (const [o,e] of saved) o.userData.enemy = e; // not necessary but safe
```
But cloning `part` also clones its userData; the root group userData contains `parts` array (circular too! root.userData.parts = [torso,head,...]) and flashMats etc. However we clone sub-parts (torso, head, arm...), not root, and those groups' userData may be default {}. The meshes have `{zone, enemy}`. clone.deep for meshes: Object3D.copy -> JSON stringify userData with enemy -> circular. Clear enemy fixes.

But also `part.updateWorldMatrix` and `new Box3().setFromObject(part)` before clone. Fine.

Better approach: avoid Object3D.clone's userData serialization entirely by writing a custom clone:
```
const clone = part.clone(true);
```
It's easy to clear enemy, and then clone. Let's implement:
```
part.traverse(o => { if (o.userData) { delete o.userData.enemy; } });
const clone = part.clone(true);
```
We can delete permanently; the zombie is dead. Note head/torso groups have userData maybe `zone`? The merged meshes have zone. Fine.

But wait — the error occurred at `Dn.copy` (Object3D.copy) from clone, due to userData circular. Clearing should fix.

Also note `clone.matrix.copy(part.matrixWorld); clone.matrix.decompose(...)` after clone sets position/quaternion/scale to world; good. But since parts are children of root (scene), matrixWorld includes root transform. clone added directly to scene. Good.

Let me fix. Then retry the kill. Also the debug eval returned an error after the zombie was removed? In killEnemy, `z.dead=true`, removed from state and scene, then createRagdoll threw, so no ragdoll and no blood. The kill didn't complete scoring etc. The zombie is gone from state. Fine, reload after fix.

Let me apply the fix.

